  "scenes": [
    {"text": "How does air circulate around the Earth? Let's draw it step by step.", "durationSeconds": 4},
    {"text": "Air pressure forms belts. Low at the equator, high near thirty degrees, low near sixty, and high at the poles.", "durationSeconds": 7},
    {"text": "Winds blow between the belts. The trade winds come from the east, the westerlies from the west, and polar easterlies circle the poles.", "durationSeconds": 7},
    {"text": "Mountains matter too. Moist air from the sea rises up the slope, cools and drops rain. Sinking air leaves the far side dry.", "durationSeconds": 7},
    {"text": "All of this weather happens in the troposphere, the lowest layer of the atmosphere.", "durationSeconds": 6},
    {"text": "And the belts shape climates: rainforests under the equatorial low, deserts under the subtropical highs.", "durationSeconds": 7}
  ],
